            <ArticleTitle>Retinal ganglion cell death in glaucoma: Exploring the role of neuroinflammation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>134-42</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ejphar.2016.03.064</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0014-2999(16)30204-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In clinical glaucoma, as well as in experimental models, the loss of retinal ganglion cells occurs by apoptosis. This final event is preceded by inflammatory responses involving the activation of innate and adaptive immunity, with retinal and optic nerve resident glial cells acting as major players. Here we review the current literature on the role of neuroinflammation in neurodegeneration, focusing on the inflammatory molecular mechanisms involved in the pathogenesis and progression of the optic neuropathy.</AbstractText>
